@TommyBoi096 Not really, the company needs money, not now but gradually. A warrant overhang as you call it simply postpones the next needed share issuance. 

I am not in the camp that is very hostile to placements including say 0.5w 12-18 m out 40% above the issuance price of the share.

Instead I think it is quite smart.

@on_Temu A lot, a lot... comes down to if you think it's a project that the company's mgt is qualified to permit, finance and technically put into production. It also has to do with the country.

Yes, I have looked at them long time ago, I am not up to date, but I feel a bit lost when it comes to permit issues in Brazil. Not too confident in my knowledge about this company's situation or ability to get it right.

I am very confident NCAU can permit, finance and technically manage this heap leach production in Ghana.

@gopatsgoat1 Well, you and I would probably not agree what is a subsidy and what is not.  

I see all these US/CA investors hugging fossile fuel and hating renewables for political reasons.

I am strongly convinced I would be a lot poorer if I let things like that cloud my thinking.

@Henrik115 @RealStevenFat1 @mindcriticalthe What an increadible incentive to increase batteries.

The best cure for low prices is low prices. Always.

Probably also very positive for wind as wind often blows the most when the sun doesn't shine and therefore reduces the battery needs.

@Henrik115 It seems clear to me a lot of energy will be needed. And equally clear that is not from nuclear anytime soon. 

So I guess I take the opposite view on you comment on especially silver. I think solar has only started and that batteries will be a huge component.

Eoulus battery adventures in the US are interesting to me. I am guessing the Chinese will do a lot of battery (a lot cheaper than US versions) and wind solutions as well as keeping older coal and gas plants for flexibility.

@Sunsoarinv @Johansson11111 @TheGruvfyllo Mitt argument mot dem för 1 vecka sedan hade varit att i en värld av fallande oljepriser och stigande guldpriser så minskar fördelen för royalty bolagen (med det motiverad premium)

I skrivande stund så hade det argumentet inte åldrats så väl, så absolut, inget fel på streaming bolag. 

Personligen har jag dock bara ett $VROY Vizsla Royalties. Men det är mer en spekulation att de är en uppköpskandidat innan året är slut.

@yhgvdb Well, mining has risks everywhere, without exception. As far as I know K92 they are in a good area.

Rick Rule is one of the people I listen most to. But the funny thing is that perhaps 10% of what he is fond of fits me. The rest I consider too high risk :-) 

And I have looked at a lot of what he likes.

@Grazes I think most of the PM stocks I own have the capability to double by end of 2026 given unchanged metal prices. This due to startups and expansions.

Mako is not an exception to that.

While I don't think this is an especially aggressive call, one should remember that there can often be violent corrections now and then, accidents can happen and I can simply be completely wrong.

@DGoodall49886 Obviously extremely forward looking, but you got a comment on them thinking about expanding the option mill from 350 tpd to 1000 tpd. This would obviously be after project 1 is cashflowing. I would simply compare the capacity of the P1 mill and those 1k tpd.

Perhaps assuming a bit lower grades though. All in all that would put us at say 25-30m from those two mills say year 2-5. To move further towards 50m they need to drill out more resources/reserves and build "project 2" at Copala.

Again, very forward looking, but I love them having and sharing the longer term goals.

@ChrisKonstrukt Aha, sorry, but then you are wrong about 2 years etc. They are entering production now, the CAPEX is done from a plant perspective. Stacking of ore starts this summer. 
As the plans are not supported by 43-101 essays they cannot really talk about expected costs and growth plans.

I recommend watching the video carefully and trying to read between the lines...

@ChrisKonstrukt You have the PEA as the most current indication. 
https://t.co/hzTja5tI5V

Stated company goal is to stay on these numbers as they progress towards pre FS and BFS. Always wise to assume some inflation though.

@AveCaesar511 Yes, very ambitious growth story. Very likely GoldBull was not their last purchase.

I like heap leach growth stories with infrastructure in Nevada &amp; Arizona.

Again, the video should be viewed also from an educational perspective, also by Mako shareholders.

@tellis998 That is a good point that I agree with. 

However, the difference now might be that the US, having the strongest military, actually aims for a cheaper currency?
So is the military strength really about automatically getting a strong currency, or is it about getting what you want?

@_Jones888 Just to be clear, SEK is up a lot. But no, I am almost always fully invested so if I want to increase somewhere I have to make hard decisions on other holdings.

SEK is actually of little importance to me except that my account value is measured in it. 

Now more than ever, all currencies are questionable (even US citizens will have to start thinking...) for more and more reasons imo. So.. gold makes a lot of sense.

@tomersiso Yes, I agree on the company but hesitate on the larger trends that impact the company and its shareprice short term. 

It simply cannot compete at all with the risk reward I see in gold/silver right now.

But that can change quickly for many reasons. Including a massive run up in PM stocks.

@tomersiso Especially clean tanker managements have fantastic opportunities to make money right now.

Sell ships and buy back shares priced at half of NAV. 

But if they have that low valuation, a liquid market to sell/buy, and not doing that, then they are crappy mangements imo.

@AveCaesar511 Not really, they are small holdings for now, 1-2% and I am still learning about them.

My highest convictions in the space are $VZLA and $KNT. 

And below that obviously well communicated names like $GGD, $MKO, $BOGO, $GOT

@momtchil74 @DavidNordstrm7 @andr_nordstrom @AndersSjastad So besides being a value investor, I also want to be reasonably within either what I think could be a negative trend at its final stages or within a positive trend.

I keep exposure to large tankers because I am hoping we are at the end of a negative trend. I am prepared to increase further to shipping if I get a strong sense that is the case. But right now, I am weighted as I see my best chance for rapid portfolio growth going forward. 

But those choices and thoughts are constantly evolving and I try to communicate it in some way through top position lists etc here.

@sardinesforsale Not really, more holding about 1% of my portfolio and doing some trading. 

My main thinking is that I want maximum exposure to $VZLA which is through the common stock. I also love the liquidity in the common stock as I do trade it. 

(But... will be increasingly difficult trade selling on strength as I so strongly feel this will be the key driver of my portfolio result coming years).

@HacheurC @Shipping_VIE Yes, still there you also have other weaknesses such as the share older overvalued non ECO ships with inferior fuel economy. 

Okeanis of course also have a solid track record of outperforming DHT.

Finally DHT is very expensive vs OET as the share has been pushed up by Scorpio.

@Toronto242M To quote Rick Rule, in mining you are either a contrarian or a victim. 

Same in shipping, when technical traders sell because they see something in the chart, that is typically where I am a buyer from the mainstream. 

That is my experience. Yours might be different.

@Nirvana12319 Well, I don't mind being challenged. What I will do is to try to follow up with further posts soon (based on the PEA) and when the FS study is out why I think it will not be difficult for them to hit 8 usd AISC the first 4 years. 

Basically it is simple math when we live in a world that gives higher NPV if you use the high grade first (if you can, as $VZLA can). 

I do agree that in mining it is not uncommon that companies fail to deliver what FS studies indicate. That we will not know until 2028 and onwards being the first full years of production.

@Nirvana12319 Well, it is all about the grades. I recommend you to look at the recent PEA and the video comments about the upcoming FS based on the new mineral resource.

The highest grade comes out first and a lot will even be stockpiled before the startup by these extremely experienced people that built SilverCrest.

However, again, it is my base case, I could be wrong in the end. I don't like being wrong publically so I try not to make aggressive calls.

@sardinesforsale I have no strong opinion on copper. To be honest I am still looking and trying to get a grip if the China bears or those that admire China, who is right.

That will determine copper coming years I think.

I am not up to date on ERO.

@radog_77 @1_adoy McEwen picked up $GOT.V (slightly) earlier than me..

And I think I found them, and contacted management, quite early, or at least well timed. 

And certainly in a different gold environment.

@momtchil74 I think it is similar to TEN but perhaps slightly more positive as one can have hope the main owner could some day do the right things while TEN's CEO seems to be ideologically against doing what I think is a slam dunk when valued at a fraction of NAV ie buybacks.

Maybe.. I would say that I think Navios leadership is also better at the core business.

I do own some TEN but no NMM right now.

@AxtellTyler I have been buying up a lot of the $VROY others have been selling. That is why I have been very quiet about it.

Imo, this is the highest quality silver royalty out there. Increadibly attractive and the signal I have gotten from mgt is that they are open to sell it to highest bidder as they build the mine. Meaning probably in 18 months or so. 

I can only begin to imagine how competitive that process will be from the large royalty companies that want a higher silver proportion.
